# Copy to deploy/.env and set the value for THIS host and THIS environment.
#
# deploy/.env is gitignored, so it does not travel with a clone or a pull — a
# fresh checkout has no copy of it, and `docker compose -f deploy/…` reads
# `.env` from the directory it is *run in*, not from deploy/. Both of those
# together mean a deploy that looks completely normal can silently start the
# receiver with no trusted proxy at all. Always pass --env-file:
#
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/.env -f deploy/compose.nonprod.yml up -d --build
#
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS is the subnet of this site's Docker network, not any one
# address on it:
#
#   docker network inspect henley-website-<env>-net -f '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Subnet}}{{end}}'
#
# On 2026-10-06 that was 192.168.176.0/20 for henley-website-prod-net
# (deploy/.env.prod) and 192.168.160.0/20 for henley-website-nonprod-net
# (deploy/.env). The subnet survives host reboots and NPM recreations; it
# changes only if the network itself is deleted and recreated. The addresses
# on it are not kept: the 2026-09-27 reboot gave npm-attachment a different one.
# A bare address still works and means that one host. Entries are
# comma-separated, and one that is neither an address nor a network stops the
# receiver starting.
#
# Check it took. This prints what the receiver will believe, the warning that
# it will believe nothing, or why it refused to start:
#
#   docker logs henley-website-forms-<env> 2>&1 | grep -E 'believed|TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS' | tail -1
TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=
